            <ArticleTitle>Stomach perforation post cardiopulmonary resuscitation-A case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>43-46</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2210-2612(17)30420-0</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijscr.2017.08.028</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Stomach perforation after cardiopulmonary resuscitation is a rare finding. This is mainly caused by incorrect management of the airway during CPR performed by non-medical personnel.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PRESENTATION OF CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report a case of 72year old female who sustained a stomach perforation during prolonged CPR in an out of hospital arrest situation. This was diagnosed on a computed tomography scan of the abdomen requiring midline laparotomy and a primary repair of the stomach.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The training of medical and non-medical persons in cardiopulmonary resuscitation is to be encouraged. However it should be emphasized that any technique which breaches the normal integrity of the body can itself lead to life-threatening complications.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A high degree of vigilance is required in patients presenting after a cardiac arrest and CPR.</AbstractText>
